];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Why does the Tower of London keep ravens?', a: 'The Tower keeps at least six ravens because of a legend that "if the ravens of the Tower of London are lost or fly away, the Crown and the Tower itself will fall." This legend is not documented before the mid-20th century; it is not a medieval tradition. The ravens are maintained today as a cultural and tourist institution by Historic Royal Palaces.' },
  { q: 'How old is the Tower of London raven tradition?', a: 'The first documentary evidence of Tower of London ravens as a managed tradition dates to around the 1890s. The specific "doom prophecy" (Crown will fall if ravens leave) is not found in any source before the 1940s–1950s. It was likely developed or formalised during or after World War II, possibly with Churchill\'s involvement.' },
